  "slots": {
    "question": [
      {
        "claim": "How does regional political climate moderate the relationship between political conservatism and COVID-19 vaccine hesitancy and uptake?",
        "quotes": [
          "The goal of the present research was to examine how political climate moderates the relationship between self-reported political conservatism and COVID-19 vaccine hesitancy and uptake. We collected online survey data from 683 participants between March 8 and April 19, 2023. Controlling for age, education, income, and race, there was an interaction between political conservatism and conservative political climate for both vaccine and booster hesitancy (β = .07, p = .03; β = .12, p < .001, respectively), such that liberals were less likely to be hesitant regardless of political climate. However, conservatives living in liberal political climates were less vaccine hesitant than their conservative counterparts living in conservative regions. A similar interaction was for the likelihood of receiving a COVID-19 booster (OR =.84, p = .049). Liberals were more likely to receive a booster regardless of political climate, while conservatives’ likelihood was associated with their political climate. Observed patterns linking liberal political climates with vaccine uptake among conservative individuals have important implications for vaccination efforts among conservative individuals in the United States.",
          "page": 1
        ]
      }
    ],
    "central_claim": [
      {
        "claim": "The interaction between political conservatism and conservative political climate significantly moderates the relationship between political orientation and COVID-19 vaccine hesitancy and booster uptake, with conservative individuals in liberal political climates being less hesitant and more likely to receive boosters than those in conservative climates.",
        "quotes": [
          "However, conservatives living in liberal political climates were less vaccine hesitant than their conservative counterparts living in conservative regions. A similar interaction was for the likelihood of receiving a COVID-19 booster (OR =.84, p = .049). Liberals were more likely to receive a booster regardless of political climate, while conservatives’ likelihood was associated with their political climate.",
          "page": 1
        ]
      }
    ],
    "evidence": [
      {
        "claim": "The study used an online survey of 683 U.S. participants collected between March 8 and April 19, 2023, with data on political conservatism (measured via social and economic conservatism scales), regional political climate (based on 2020 presidential election vote share by zip code), and self-reported vaccine and booster hesitancy and status.",
        "quotes": [
          "We collected online survey data from 683 participants between March 8 and April 19, 2023. Controlling for age, education, income, and race, there was an interaction between political conservatism and conservative political climate for both vaccine and booster hesitancy (β = .07, p = .03; β = .12, p < .001, respectively), such that liberals were less likely to be hesitant regardless of political climate. However, conservatives living in liberal political climates were less vaccine hesitant than their conservative counterparts living in conservative regions.",
          "page": 1
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Political conservatism and regional political climate were measured using self-reported items and zip code-based election data, respectively, with regression and logistic models controlling for age, gender, education, income, race, and perceived vaccine access.",
        "quotes": [
          "Political conservatism (i.e., a political ideology that emphasizes tradition, authority, and limited government intervention) was assessed across two items. The first item, assessing social conservatism, asked participants to indicate their political orientation on 'social issues (for example, abortion, gun rights, gay rights)'. The second item, assessing economic conservatism, asked participants to indicate their political orientation on 'economic issues (for example, taxation, government spending)'. Both items were rated on an 11-point sliding scale from strongly liberal (0) to strongly conservative (10). These two items were averaged to form a political conservatism scale.",
          "page": 2
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The study employed multiple regression and logistic regression models to assess the interaction between political conservatism and regional political climate on vaccine and booster hesitancy and uptake, with key findings reported in Tables 3–6.",
        "quotes": [
          "Multiple linear regression models were built to assess the association between political conservatism and conservative regional climate with vaccine hesitancy, controlling for age, gender, education, income, race, and perceived vaccine access. Ordinary least squares estimation was used with default standard errors. Model 1 included only control variables age, gender, education, income, race, and vaccine access as dependent variables. Model 2 included control variables and political conservatism and conservative regional climate. Model 3 included all variables from Model 2 and the interaction between political conservatism and conservative regional climate. This interaction assessed the extent to which the association between political conservatism and vaccine attitudes/behavior was moderated by conservative regional climate.",
          "page": 4
        ]
      }
    ],
    "key_findings": [
      {
        "claim": "Conservatives living in liberal political climates were less vaccine hesitant than conservatives in conservative climates, indicating that liberal environments reduce vaccine hesitancy among politically conservative individuals.",
        "quotes": [
          "However, conservatives living in liberal political climates were less vaccine hesitant than their conservative counterparts living in conservative regions.",
          "page": 1
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Liberals were more likely to receive a booster regardless of political climate, while conservative individuals' booster uptake was strongly influenced by regional political climate.",
        "quotes": [
          "Liberals were more likely to receive a booster regardless of political climate, while conservatives’ likelihood was associated with their political climate.",
          "page": 1
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The interaction between political conservatism and regional political climate was significant for both vaccine and booster hesitancy, with the effect being stronger in more conservative regions.",
        "quotes": [
          "There was a significant interaction between political conservatism and conservative regional climate for vaccine hesitancy, booster hesitancy, and booster status. The interaction for vaccine hesitancy indicates that at the lowest levels of conservatism (i.e., liberalism) vaccine hesitancy is lower regardless of regional climate.",
          "page": 9
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "For booster status, the most liberal participants were over 65% likely to get a booster regardless of climate, while conservatives in liberal climates were 60% likely to get boosted, compared to less than 35% in conservative climates.",
        "quotes": [
          "The most politically liberal individuals, were over 65% likely to get boosted regardless of climate. The most conservative participants in liberal climates were 60% likely to get boosted, while conservatives in conservative climates were less than 35% likely to get boosted (OR =.83, p = .026).",
          "page": 8
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The interaction between political conservatism and regional political climate was not significant for vaccine status, suggesting that other factors (e.g., mandates) may override the influence of political climate on vaccination behavior.",
        "quotes": [
          "The interaction for vaccination was not significant, this difference between attitudes (hesitancy) and action (vaccination), likely reflects the high prevalence of COVID-19 vaccine mandates.",
          "page": 10
        ]
      }
    ],
    "scope_and_limits": [
      {
        "claim": "The study relies on self-reported data, which may be subject to social desirability and recall bias, and the sample is limited to White and Black participants recruited through an online platform, with potential bias toward individuals with internet access.",
        "quotes": [
          "Self-report is susceptible to social desirability [30] and recall bias [31], and we cannot assume that reported vaccine status reflects actual vaccine status. Additionally, our sample was recruited through an online platform and only includes participants with access to this platform. Further, our sample includes only White and Black participants recruited through Qualtrics Panels, and may not generalize to broader populations in the United States.",
          "page": 10
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The study uses 2020 presidential election results as a proxy for regional political climate, which may not fully capture the current political atmosphere or other regional factors (e.g., population density) that could influence vaccine behavior.",
        "quotes": [
          "Because we used 2020 presidential election votes by zip code as a proxy for political climate, it must be noted that other zip code-based differences could be driving the associations noted in this paper. Regional factors (e.g., population density) may play a role in linking 2020 election votes to vaccine attitudes and behaviors.",
          "page": 10
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The study is cross-sectional and cannot establish causality or directionality between political climate, political orientation, and vaccine behavior.",
        "quotes": [
          "This data is cross-sectional and we cannot assume causal or directional associations.",
          "page": 10
        ]
      }
    ],
    "theoretical_positioning": [
      {
        "claim": "The paper situates itself within the existing literature on political behavior and public health, extending prior findings by examining how political climate moderates the effect of individual political orientation on vaccine behavior, rather than treating political orientation or climate in isolation.",
        "quotes": [
          "The present research provides the first assessment of the potential interaction between political orientation, political climate, and COVID vaccination. Additionally, by looking at both COVID vaccine and booster status and hesitancy, we identify how attitudes and behaviors may have shifted as we transitioned from initial vaccine doses to boosters.",
          "page": 8
        ]
      }
    ],
    "interlocutor_claim": [
      {
        "claim": "The paper attributes to prior research (e.g., Rönn et al., 2023) a positive association between Democratic vote share and higher vaccination rates, which the current study builds upon by showing that this effect is stronger for politically conservative individuals in liberal climates.",
        "quotes": [
          "At both the county and the state level there was a positive association between Democratic vote share (i.e., % of people voting for the Democratic candidate) in the 2020 presidential election and COVID-19 vaccination rates from 2021−2022 [19].",
          "page": 2
        ]
      }
    ]
  }
}
